### What
Be able to use `IServiceProvider` to resolve a dependency within an `services.Configure<MyOptions>()` registration.

### Why
Currently the only way to resolve a dependency inside of `ConfigureServices` you will need to create an intermediate `IServiceProvider` using the BuildServiceProvider() method. The big issue with this, apart from performance, is that different instances of the service provider can give different singleton services.

### How
Enables the user to configure an options instance using a factory that exposes the `IServiceProvider` instance.
Making it possible to lazily resolve dependencies inside an options class.

### Examples

	public class MyOptions
	{
		public double Threshold { get; set; }
	}

	public static IServiceCollection ConfigureServices(this IServiceCollection services)
	{
		return services
			.AddSingleton<Random>()

			// #1 Resolving dependency inside of an options configurator (by creating an intermediate service provider(
			.Configure<MyOptions>((opt) =>
			{
				var sp = services.BuildServiceProvider();
				var random = sp.GetRequiredService<Random>(),
				opt.Threshold = random.NextDouble();
			})

			// #2 You could use the `AddSingleton` method directly (which is how `Configure` works) and passing the service provider
			.AddSingleton<IConfigureOptions<ApiKeyAuthenticationOptions>>(
				(sp) => new ConfigureNamedOptions<ApiKeyAuthenticationOptions>("", (o) =>
				{
				    o.Threshold = sp.GetRequiredService<Random>().NextDouble();
				})
			)

			// #3 This variant is a result of this PR, allowing for a much simpler syntax
			.Configure<MyOptions>((opt, sp) =>
			{
				var random = sp.GetRequiredService<Random>(),
				opt.Threshold = random.NextDouble();
			})
	}


NOTE: That _alternative 2_ is what is actually happening behind the scenes when writing the _alternative 3_. 
It is just easier to read, more future-proof and it also resembles the classic _alternative 1_

### Risks
No breaking changes, this would extend the API surface.

